{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310710,"nonce":"4t7pjH+AJ11TvPFc6kKQiRKGBf8=","title":"A static calling another static in the SAME class of an import-compiled module traps at runtime (exit 133, no compile error) — found ...","message":"Found 2026-07-28 rewriting `nue.tokenizer.BPETokenizer.encodeBPE` to O(n log n).\n\nWritten the obvious way — `static heapPush()` calling `static heapLess()`, both\nprivate to the same class — nue **trapped at runtime with exit 133** (and 139 on\nother prompts). NO compile error, NO E0100, and `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` showed\nall three functions with REAL pointers in both the Cranelift and LLVM lists, so\nit is not the [[bugs_trap_stub_cascade]] stranded-function shape either.\n\n**Fix: make them INSTANCE methods and inline the comparison.** Instance dispatch\nis what every other method in that class already uses, and it works.\n\n**REVISED ATTRIBUTION (same day, after reading the compiler).** I first filed\nthis under [[bugs_import_xmodule_member_resolution]]. The evidence now points\nsomewhere else — TIER-BOUNDARY LINKAGE, not static resolution:\n\n- `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` on the failing build showed `heapPush` and `heapPop`\n  with **`[fn-ptr-llvm]`** entries but `heapLess` with only a **`[fn-ptr]`**\n  (Cranelift) entry. So two LLVM-promoted functions were calling a callee that\n  never entered the LLVM module.\n- `llvm_jit_backend.rs` `finalize()` calls `add_global_mapping` ONLY for\n  `self.runtime_symbols` (the C runtime). A Haxe function left on Cranelift that\n  a promoted LLVM function calls gets no mapping and no definition — which is\n  exactly the shape that traps.\n- The static call resolver itself\n  (`resolve_function_id_with_qualified_fallback`, hir_to_mir.rs:1717) is sound:\n  SymbolId, then FQN in the local map, then FQN in the external map, then\n  `external_function_name_map` by qualified name. No bare-name widening.\n\n**NOT PROVEN.** Confirming it needs a tier comparison between the failing and\nworking builds, and the obvious tool is unusable: **`RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`\nmakes a clean build SIGILL (132)**, so the diagnostic perturbs the run. Any\nfuture attempt should first fix or work around that.\n\nIf the tier theory holds, the rule is not \"avoid static-\u003estatic\" but \"a promoted\nfunction's callees must be promoted, inlined, or given a global mapping\" — and\ninstance methods only fixed it by changing which functions got promoted.\n\n**Rule of thumb: in an import-compiled module, prefer instance methods. A\nstatic-\u003estatic call is suspect even within one class.**\n\nSix wrong hypotheses, each disproven with a standalone repro (~10 s per compile\nvs ~60 s per model run — build the standalone FIRST):\n1. move semantics on `Array` args — WRONG, arrays mutate through calls fine\n2. `String` field assigned into an array slot — WRONG, works\n3. shared-string aliasing (`pieces[a] = merges[r].merged` corrupting the table)\n   — WRONG, table survived two full runs intact\n4. `while (true)` loop lowering — WRONG, bounded form still trapped\n5. `Array\u003cBool\u003e` — WRONG\n6. trap stub / unresolved symbol — WRONG, pointers present\n\nWhat actually localised it: reproducing the algorithm standalone (it worked in\nevery shape, including instance-\u003estatic), which proved the defect was the *nue\nimport-compiled context*, not the code. Then the static-\u003estatic shape was the\nonly remaining difference.\n\nSee [[bugs_extern_optional_arg_supplied_breaks_codegen]] — same family of\nSILENT, runtime-only, no-compile-error defects. When nue traps with no\ndiagnostic, suspect the module/linkage shape before the algorithm.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_static_to_static_call_traps_in_import_module` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}